Title: Erg struct: schema-literal fields, drop the headers map bag
Created: 2026-05-11
Author: claude-sonnet-4-6

--- log ---
2026-05-11T00:00Z claude-sonnet-4-6 created

--- body ---
## Context
This is the unfinished half of ticket 0105. PR #115 added accessor methods
and unexported the headers field, but the struct itself still stores values
in a `map[string][]string` bag and the accessors do runtime string-keyed
lookups. The struct does not mirror the spec's header table; rule checks
on a typo like `"Blocked-By"` (capital B) would silently miss values.

The spec defines a closed set of 6 headers (spec-erg-v1.md §Headers):
Title, Created, Author, Closed, Blocked-by, Tags. Each is fully specified
(required, repeatable, type). The struct should reflect this directly.

## Current shape (verbatim, erg.go:121-135)
```go
type Erg struct {
    Path     string
    headers  map[string][]string
    LogLines []string
    Body     string
    HasMagic bool
    LogSepCount  int
    BodySepCount int
    ClosedInLog  bool
    ClosedInBody bool
}
```

The `headers` map does two jobs in tension:
1. Storing values for the 6 known headers (called by accessors)
2. Recording trace of all keys seen, for validator rules 2-3 (required,
   unknown, repeated-singleton)

## Proposed shape
```go
type Erg struct {
    Path     string
    HasMagic bool

    // v1 headers — typed fields populated from first occurrence
    Title     string
    Created   string
    Author    string
    Closed    string    // value, may be empty
    ClosedSet bool      // true if `Closed:` line appeared at all
    BlockedBy []string  // repeatable
    Tags      []string  // repeatable

    LogLines []string
    Body     string
    LogSepCount  int
    BodySepCount int

    // Diagnostic trace (validator-only; never read by accessors)
    Unknown            []string  // unknown header keys seen (e.g. "Status")
    RepeatedSingletons []string  // singleton keys seen more than once
    ClosedInLog        bool
    ClosedInBody       bool
}
```

`ClosedSet` is needed because validator rule 4 distinguishes "header
absent" from "header present with empty value". A `*string` would work
too but is awkward at call sites.

## Behaviour preservation
| Today                                          | After                                                  |
|------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|
| `t.Title()` reads `headers["Title"][0]`        | `t.Title` direct field                                 |
| `t.BlockedBy()` reads `headers["Blocked-by"]`  | `t.BlockedBy` direct field                             |
| `t.Tags()` trims and filters                   | parser stores already-trimmed non-empty values         |
| `t.Closed()` checks path OR header non-empty   | unchanged method body, reads `t.Closed`                |
| `t.ClosedHeader()` returns `Closed != ""`      | returns `t.ClosedSet && strings.TrimSpace(Closed)!=""` |
| validate rule 2: `_, ok := headers[hdr]`       | `if t.Title == "" || t.Created == "" || t.Author == ""` (tightens: empty value now also fails — spec implies non-empty) |
| validate rule 3 unknown: walk map keys         | walk `t.Unknown`                                       |
| validate rule 3 singleton: `len(vals) > 1`     | walk `t.RepeatedSingletons`                            |
| validate rule 3a Tags values: walk `headers["Tags"]` | walk `t.Tags`                                    |
| validate rule 4 Closed empty: walk `headers["Closed"]` | `if t.ClosedSet && strings.TrimSpace(t.Closed) == ""` |

The required-header check changes semantics slightly: currently a
`Title:` line with empty value passes rule 2 (key present). After
refactor, empty value also fails. The spec implies non-empty values for
required headers; tightening is correct. Document in PR body and add a
test.

## Call sites
Accessors (Title, Closed, BlockedBy, Tags, ClosedHeader, Created, Author,
BlockedByRefs) are called from 6 files. Strategy:
- Keep the accessor methods as-is (one-line readers of typed fields) so
  call sites do not change. The method shape becomes trivial; we could
  inline later but doing so expands the diff.
- The 4 direct `t.headers[...]` accesses in validate.go switch to the
  new fields.

Accessor call sites (no change to call code):
- archive.go: Closed, BlockedByRefs, ClosedHeader
- check.go:   ClosedHeader, Closed (×2), BlockedByRefs
- close.go:   Closed (×2), BlockedBy
- ready.go:   Closed (×2), Tags, BlockedByRefs, Title
- validate.go: Created, BlockedByRefs (×2) — plus 4 direct map accesses to rewrite

## Test impact
- erg_test.go: parser tests construct Erg literals; update to new shape.
- validate_test.go: rule 2/3/4 fixtures may need adjustment if they
  exercise the "present but empty" required-header edge case.
- Add a test asserting compile-time type safety: a misspelled-key access
  must be impossible (the file simply does not compile).

## Out of scope
- Parser/validator merge into a single pass (the secondary 0105 ask).
  Split into a follow-up ticket if desired — different risk surface.
- Renaming `Erg.Tags()` → `Erg.Tag()`. That is ticket 0106's territory.
  If 0106 has landed by the time this is implemented, propagate; otherwise
  keep `Tags`.
- Splitting `parseErg` into smaller functions; only struct shape changes.

## Dependencies
- Soft dep: ticket 0115 (split Ref into ref.go). If 0115 lands first,
  this ticket touches only the Erg-related portion of erg.go. If this
  lands first, 0115 needs a trivial rebase.
- If 0106 has landed: rename Tags field → Tag here too.

## Exit criteria
- `Erg` struct fields correspond 1-to-1 to the v1 header table.
- No runtime string-keyed map access in accessors or validator for known
  headers; only the `Unknown` slice carries unknown-key data.
- `make test` passes.
- PR body documents the rule-2 tightening (empty required header rejected).
